### B.3 Cause of Cycles

The auto-decomposer, when decomposing a task, creates forward links (parent -> child) AND also creates reverse links (child -> parent) for each new child. This is the mechanism by which children are marked as "contributing to" their parent. However, when the parent task (`t_806b38ea`) was later decomposed into children (`t_4e1e5d6b`, `t_bae47195`, `t_d2f82641`), the decomposition process also linked those children back to the already-existing parent — but the parent was ALSO the root that was being decomposed. The result: the children are parents of their own parent.

**This is a graph anomaly.** Whether it causes operational problems depends on how the dispatcher traverses the graph. If the dispatcher only follows forward links (parent -> child) for decomposition and reverse links (child -> parent) for completion propagation, the cycles may be harmless. If the dispatcher performs graph traversal without cycle detection, infinite loops are possible.

