## IX. INVARIANCE VS MULTIPLE REALIZATION

### Logical Distinction

| Property | Definition | Experimental Evidence Required |
|----------|------------|-------------------------------|
| **Representation invariance** | G is preserved under invertible transformations of V | Show that isomorphic graphs produce equivalent predictions |
| **Decomposition robustness** | G is preserved under variable merging/splitting | Show that different decompositions produce equivalent predictions |
| **Measurement invariance** | Predictions are preserved under changes in measurement | Show that different observation functions produce equivalent predictions |
| **Implementation invariance** | Predictions are preserved under different implementations | Show that different implementations produce equivalent predictions |
| **Multiple realization** | Different physical systems can instantiate the same G | Show that different substrates produce equivalent predictions |

### Critical Finding

**These are logically distinct properties.** A system can have one without the others:
- A system can be representation-invariant but not implementation-invariant.
- A system can be implementation-invariant but not decomposition-robust.
- A system can have multiple realizations without being measurement-invariant.

**A single successful implementation cannot support a multiple-realization claim.** Multiple realization requires at least **two different implementations** that produce equivalent predictions.

**A single invariance test cannot support a general invariance claim.** Each invariance property requires its own experimental test.

